{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Activate Backlash}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Activate_Backlash}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Activate Backlash
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 
\par This menu lets you switch \ul Stage Backlash\plain\fs24 {\v 88HTWS@Sem.HLP} ON. or OFF.
\par 
\par \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\tab If you \ul click\plain\fs24 {\v CLICK@Sem.HLP} ON it is marked with \{bmc bm1.bmp\}. Backlash correction is then performed after each stage movement.
\par 
\par \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\tab Clicking on \b Do Backlash \plain\fs24 causes the stage to execute a backlash correction operation.
\par 
\par Backlash correction is defined in \uldb \b Preference Selection Stage\plain\b\fs24 {\v 3TQY_S@Errors.HLP}\plain\fs24  tab.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Centre Feature}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Centre_Feature}
\pard\keepn\tx355 {\up K}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up K} Activate Backlash}
\b\fs36\cf1 Centre Feature
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 This function enables the user to select an area of the image with the mouse. This area will be positioned in the centre of the image area by moving the stage and/or the beam in the appropriate direction. The magnification is increased so that the area fills the field of view.
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 \{bmc bm2.bmp\}\tab Call \b Stage\plain\fs24  from the \uldb \b Menu Bar\plain\b\fs24 {\v 8ZP0K4@Sem.HLP}\plain\fs24  with \b <ALT + G>\plain\fs24  and \ul click\plain\fs24 {\v CLICK@Sem.HLP} on \b Centre Feature\plain\fs24  .
\par \{bmc bm3.bmp\}\tab Use \b <CTRL + SHIFT + TAB>\plain\fs24  from the keyboard to activate the function.
\par 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-6 \cellx7015\cellx7515\cellx12835\pard\intbl\tx355 \{bmc bm4.bmp\}\tab When the function is activated, the cursor is changed to a cross. Point to the top left corner of the feature field and \ul drag\plain\fs24 {\v DRAG@LEO_1500.HLP} a rectangle around the feature. 
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl When the mouse button is released the feature area will be moved to the centre, the magnification is increased and the function terminates. 
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl The cursor is switched back to it's normal appearance.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl To abort the function click the right mouse button.\cell\pard \pard\intbl\tx355 \cell\pard \pard\intbl\tx355 \{bmc bm5.bmp\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 The range of centration and its accuracy is determined by the \uldb \b Centre Point / Feature Settings\plain\b\fs24 {\v Centre_Point___Feature_Settings}\plain\fs24 .
\par \pard\tx355 If \uldb \b Survey Mode\plain\b\fs24 {\v 4_DGYJ5@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  is active Centre Feature will also switch to \uldb \b Resolution Mode\plain\b\fs24 {\v JZNOU8@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24 
\par \pard\tx355 \{bmc bm6.bmp\}\tab The accuracy of the stage positioning for high magnification may require that \uldb \b stage backlash correction\plain\b\fs24 {\v Activate_Backlash}\plain\fs24  is on and even this may not be adequate for very high magnification.
\par 
\par \{bmc bm7.bmp\}\tab When this function is performed the initial magnification is saved, and may be restored by pressing \b <F4>\plain\fs24 .  If \uldb \b Survey Mode\plain\b\fs24 {\v Survey_Mode@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  was active when Centre Feature was performed <F4> also switches from \uldb \b Resolution Mode\plain\b\fs24 {\v JZNOU8@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  to \b Survey Mode\plain\fs24 
\par \pard\tx355 \tab \{bmc bm8.bmp\} Note that subsequent presses of \b <F4>\plain\fs24  perform Mag Table functions (until another Centre Feature function is performed).
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Centre Point}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Centre_Point}
\pard\keepn\tx355 \b\fs36\cf1 Centre Point
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 
\par \pard\tx355 This function allows the user to indicate a point on the image which will be moved into the centre of the image by moving the stage and/or the beam in the appropriate direction. 
\par \pard\tx355 For immediate operation hold down the Shift key \{bmc bm9.bmp\} and double click \{bmc bm10.bmp\} on the feature to be centered.
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 \{bmc bm2.bmp\}\tab Call \b Stage\plain\fs24  from the \uldb \b Menu Bar\plain\b\fs24 {\v 8ZP0K4@Sem.HLP}\plain\fs24  with \b <ALT + G>\plain\fs24  and click on \b Centre Point\plain\fs24  .
\par 
\par \{bmc bm3.bmp\}\tab Use \b <CTRL + TAB>\plain\fs24  from the keyboard to activate the function.
\par 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-6 \cellx8145\cellx8935\cellx11705\pard\intbl\tx355 \{bmc bm4.bmp\}\tab While the function is activated, the cursor is changed to a cross. Point to the feature to be centered and \ul click\plain\fs24 {\v CLICK@Sem.HLP}. 
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl The feature will be moved to the centre and the function terminates. 
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl The cursor is switched back to it's normal appearance.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl To abort the function click with the right mouse button.\cell\pard \pard\intbl\tx355 \cell\pard \pard\intbl\tx355 \{bmc bm11.bmp\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 The range of centralisation and its accuracy is determined by the \uldb \b Centre Point / Feature Settings\plain\b\fs24 {\v Centre_Point___Feature_Settings}\plain\fs24 .
\par \pard\tx355 If \uldb \b Survey Mode\plain\b\fs24 {\v 4_DGYJ5@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  is active Centre point will also switch to \uldb \b Resolution Mode\plain\b\fs24 {\v JZNOU8@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24 
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 \{bmc bm6.bmp\}\tab The accuracy of the stage positioning for high magnification may require that \uldb \b stage backlash correction\plain\b\fs24 {\v Activate_Backlash}\plain\fs24  is on and even this may not be adequate for very high magnification.
\par 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Centre Point / Feature Settings}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Centre_Point___Feature_Settings}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn\tx355 {\up K}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up K} Centre Feature}
\b\fs36\cf1 Centre Point / Feature Settings
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 
\par \pard\tx355 \f6 The method used to centre the image may be selected to one of the following:\f2 
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 \f6\b Stage and Beam\plain\fs24 
\par \pard\tx355 \f6 The software will attempt to use the beam shift to move the point to the centre of the screen. If the movement is outside the range of the beam shift then a stage movement will be used, with the positioning such that following the move the beam shift will be zero.\f2 
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 \f6\b Stage Only\plain\fs24 
\par \pard\tx355 \f6 The stage is moved to position the point at the centre. The beam shift remains unchanged.\f2 
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 \f6\b Beam Only\plain\fs24 
\par \pard\tx355 \f6 The software attempts to use beam shift to move the point to the centre. If the movement is outside the range of the beam shift then an error message is given and the position remains unchanged.\f2 
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 \f6 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\tab Click on the \uldb \b SEM Control Stage\plain\f6\b\fs24 {\v KH.ZCT@TabMgr.HLP}\plain\f6\fs24  tab with the right button, then select \b Centre Point / Feature\plain\f6\fs24  from the menu.\f2 
\par 
\par \f6 \{bmc bm4.bmp\}\tab Click on the method to be activated which is then marked with \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\f2 
\par 
\par \f6 \{bmc bm7.bmp\}\tab Use \b Stage Only\plain\f6\fs24  for low magnifications, \b Stage and Beam\plain\f6\fs24  for medium magnifications, and \b Beam Only\plain\f6\fs24  for high magnifications.\f2 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Compucentric Rotation}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Compucentric_Rotation}
\pard\tx355 \b\fs36\cf1 Compucentric Rotation
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 When the  stage is rotated, there is an offset in the X and Y axes (unless the beam is positioned on the mechanical centre of rotation).
\par \pard\tx355  If the stage is at a non zero tilt angle there will also be an offset in the Z axis.
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 Compucentric rotation makes these offsets to the X, Y and Z axes in response to a change of the rotation angle.
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 Available on Cartesian 95, Macro and Fjeld Stages
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 Note: On the Cartesian 95 stage use of compucentric rotation mode when the stage is tilted requires that the sample height calibration has been performed.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Compucentric Tilt}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Compucentric_Tilt}
\pard {\up K}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up K} Centre Point}
\b\fs36\cf1 Compucentric Tilt
\par \plain\fs24 When a cartesian 95 stage is tilted there is an offset in the Y and Z axes.
\par 
\par Compucentric tilt makes these offsets to the Y and Z axes in response to a change of the tilt angle.
\par 
\par This function is only available with the Cartesian 95 stage.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Defining the Stage Scan Area}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Defining_the_Stage_Scan_Area}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Defining the Stage Scan Area
\par \pard \plain\fs24    
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-6 \cellx7015\cellx13755\pard\intbl You can define the stage scan area in a number of ways:
\par\intbl \par\pard \pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 \{bmc bm12.bmp\}\b  \tab \plain\fs24 Manually enter the \b Start Coordinates\plain\fs24  and \b End Coordinates
\par\intbl  \tab \plain\fs24 (\ul click\plain\fs24 {\v CLICK@Sem.HLP} on the parameter field) and then use \uldb \b Stage Calculate Scan\plain\b\fs24 {\v Stage_Calculate_Scan}\plain\fs24 .
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \{bmc bm12.bmp\} \b \tab \plain\fs24 Manually enter the  \uldb \b Stage Scan Start Position\plain\b\fs24 {\v Stage_Scan_Fields}\plain\fs24  , \b Field Size\plain\fs24  and the number of\b  Fields\plain\fs24 .
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \{bmc bm12.bmp\}\b \tab \plain\fs24 Position the stage at the location where the scan is to start (using the \b Joystick\plain\fs24 , \uldb \b SEM Control Stage\plain\b\fs24 {\v KH.ZCT@TabMgr.HLP} \plain\fs24 tab or \uldb \b Centre Point\plain\b\fs24 {\v Centre_Point}\plain\fs24  ) then press \uldb \b Stage Scan Start Here\plain\b\fs24 {\v Stage_Scan_Start_here}\plain\fs24 . 
\par\intbl \b \tab \plain\fs24 Then position the stage at the location where the scan is to end and press \uldb \b Stage Scan ends here\plain\b\fs24 {\v Stage_Scan_End_here}\plain\fs24 .
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \{bmc bm12.bmp\} \b \tab \plain\fs24 Where the whole area to be scanned can be viewed at a suitable low magnification use \uldb \b Select Area\plain\b\fs24 {\v Select_Scan_Area}. \plain\fs24 Then set the magnification to the required value for scanning and press \b Stage calc scan.\plain\fs24 \par\pard \pard\intbl \cell\pard \pard\intbl 
\par\intbl \{bmc bm13.bmp\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard\tx355 \{bmc bm8.bmp\}\tab \b Note that the stage position represents the position of the centre of the image.
\par 
\par \page
