{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Stage Registration}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Stage_Registration}
\pard\keepn\tx355 \b\fs36\cf1 Stage Registration
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 This Software option provides the ability to set up  User Defined Co-ordinate systems.
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 The default Co-ordinate system is that of the Stage, and the Co-ordinate Store/Recall option provides for lists of points in these co-ordinates
\par \pard\tx355 This is indicated on the \uldb \b Points List Dialog\plain\b\fs24 {\v 1JKVBGQ@dlgmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  by \b Stage List = Stage 
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 An Alternative Stage list may be selected (e.g.\b Stage List = Reg 1\plain\fs24 ) which defines points in terms of a different co-ordinate system.
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 A User Defined Co-ordinate System is set up with the \uldb \b Stage Registration Dialog\plain\b\fs24 {\v 4PYQ9J@dlgmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  using a \uldb \b 2 point or 3 point registration algorithm\plain\b\fs24 {\v 9GO4AM@dlgmgr.hlp} 
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 Once the Co-ordinate system is registered points saved in this points list will be in the registered co-ordinate system.
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 If such a points list is saved to file, and on a subsequent occasion the sample inserted at a different position, performing the registration sequence will re-establish the correct relationship, enabling you to go to the position labels previously saved.
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 Similarly, while registration is valid, the \uldb \b Stage Registered Movement Dialog\plain\b\fs24 {\v 5M9CCK@dlgmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  displays the position in the User Co-ordinate system while the Joystick is used to navigate the sample. Additionally co-ordinates may be typed in to move the stage to the required Co-ordinates.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Stage Scan}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Stage_Scan}
\pard\keepn {\up K}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up K} Stage Horizontal alignment}
\b\fs36\cf1 Stage Scan
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 
\par \{bmc bm2.bmp\}\tab Call \b Stage\plain\fs24  from the \uldb \b Menu Bar\plain\b\fs24 {\v 8ZP0K4@Sem.HLP}\plain\fs24  with \b <ALT + G>\plain\fs24  and then call \b Stage Scan\plain\fs24  with \b <N>\plain\fs24 .
\par 
\par \pard\tx355 \f7\fs20 
\par \pard\tx355 \f2\fs24 Stage scan provides the facility of using the motorised stage to inspect an area of the specimen as a series of fields. This may be achieved using the \uldb \b Stage Manager\plain\b\fs24 {\v 1UF1_ZG@stgmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  (recommended) or using the facilities below for the advanced user.
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 \f6 There is a choice of four different field patterns, for this example assume there are 9 fields numbered 1 through 9.\f2 
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355   \b X Boustrophodon\tab X Raster\tab \tab    Y Boustrophodon\tab Y Raster
\par \tab \plain\fs24 1  2  3\tab \tab \tab 1  2  3\tab \tab \tab \tab 1  6  7\tab \tab \tab 1  4  7
\par \tab 6  5  4\tab \tab \tab 4  5  6\tab \tab \tab \tab 2  5  8\tab \tab \tab 2  5  8
\par \tab 7  8  9\tab \tab \tab 7  8  9\tab \tab \tab \tab 3  4  9\tab \tab \tab 3  6  9
\par \f6    \f2 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-6 \cellx7015\cellx14035\pard\intbl \f6 \{bmc bm14.bmp\} \f2 Select the \b Stage Scan\plain\fs24  type from the \ul Drop-down List\plain\fs24 {\v XYVXW3@Sem:HLP}
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl There are several methods of \uldb \b Defining the Stage Scan Area\plain\b\fs24 {\v Defining_the_Stage_Scan_Area}\plain\fs24  which include manually defining \uldb \b Stage Scan Parameters\plain\b\fs24 {\v 1MHKPV_}\plain\fs24  .
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \{bmc bm14.bmp\}\f6  \f2 Once the scan pattern is defined \uldb \b Stage Scan Commands\plain\b\fs24 {\v Stage_Scan_Commands}\plain\fs24  may be used to step through the fields. 
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl The stage scan window also provides \uldb \b Stage Scan Buttons\plain\b\fs24 {\v Stage_Scan_Buttons}\plain\fs24  
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \f6 
\par\intbl \f2 \cell\pard \pard\intbl \{bmc bm13.bmp\}\f6 \cell\intbl\row
\pard \f2 
\par \page
